# AUTHOR LINE GROUPING — a TRAILING comment on each row.
#
# MUST EXCLUDE from any bulk `gg fmt` sweep: this fixture's SUBJECT is fmt
# output, and it is written in the form the formatter must KEEP.
#
# AXIS = interior comment present, in its TRAILING costume (same source line as
# the last element of a row), crossed with rows of 2.
#
# ⚠ THE ORDERING CLAIM THIS PINS, and it is the one the row logic can silently
# get wrong: `emit_trailing_comment_after(elem_end)` attaches a comment sitting
# on `elem_end`'s SOURCE line. Mid-row that line's comment belongs to the LAST
# element on the row, not to the one being emitted — so the hook must fire only
# where the row ENDS. Fire it per element instead and `# first row` is emitted
# after `1`, splitting the row it annotates.
#
# The column the comments sit at is `align_trailing_comments`' doing, not this
# feature's: trailing comments in a run are aligned to a common column AFTER the
# walk. Element-to-line assignment is what the ruling preserves; intra-line
# padding it does not touch, in either direction.
#
# RED against the pre-change binary, measured: not a fixed point — six elements
# on six lines, each comment re-attached to whichever element happened to land
# beneath it.
from std.collections import Vector

void main():
    int y = 6
    Vector[int] xs = [
        1, 2,       # first row
        3, 4,       # second row
        5, y,
    ]
    print(xs.len())
